[
    {
        "content": "<p>As I mentioned in a recent post, a macro may produce multiple commands:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">macro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"foo\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">ident</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">ident</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">command</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\">-- prints 3</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">eval</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\">   </span><span class=\"c1\">-- prints 4</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>That's great.  Unfortunately, if I try to use <code>#info_trees</code> to see what such a macro produced, I get a bunch of unreadable output:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">info_trees</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>prints</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">failed</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">to</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pretty</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">print</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">term</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">use</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">'</span><span class=\"kn\">set_option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pp</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">rawOnError</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">true'</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">for</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">raw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">representation</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Command</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">22</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩†-⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">22</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩†</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Elab</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Command</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">elabDeclaration</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">22</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩†-⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">22</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">7</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩†</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">@</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Elab</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">Term</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">elabHole</span>\n<span class=\"bp\">...</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Is there any easy way to inspect the commands that a macro such as this generated?</p>",
        "id": 619416730,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1787791635
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>As one possible hack, I found that if I put the commands into a <code>mutual</code> block, <code>#info_trees</code> will print them:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">macro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"foo\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">ident</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"n\">ident</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">command</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">do</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"kn\">mutual</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">$</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">4</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kn\">end</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"bp\">#</span><span class=\"n\">info_trees</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">in</span>\n<span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>prints</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">MacroExpansion</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">foo</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"bp\">===&gt;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kn\">mutual</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span>\n<span class=\"w\">      </span><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">        </span><span class=\"mi\">4</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kn\">end</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>However not all commands can coexist in a mutual block, so I'd like to have a more robust solution.</p>",
        "id": 619417463,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1787792120
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>You can add</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">_</span><span class=\"n\">root_</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">null</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Lean</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">ParserDescr</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">unary</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`ppDedent</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">unary</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`many</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">binary</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`andthen</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`ppLine</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">cat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"ss\">`command</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)))</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and it will kinda work</p>",
        "id": 619478730,
        "sender_full_name": "Robin Arnez",
        "timestamp": 1787819294
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, that works fine.  Remarkable!</p>\n<p>I might argue that this definition should be present in Lean by default, unless it has some negative side effect I'm unaware of.  In any case, this solution is just fine for my purposes.  Thanks!</p>",
        "id": 619525083,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1787832788
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If this is useful: I routinely use <code>set_option trace.Elab.command true</code> to debug my macros</p>",
        "id": 619526667,
        "sender_full_name": "Théophile Wallez",
        "timestamp": 1787833204
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Théophile: Thanks - I know about that command, but usually I use <code>#info_trees in</code> because it's more convenient for showing the output of a single macro invocation, not all macros in my program.  In any case, <del><code>set_option trace.Elab.command true</code> won't show multiple commands generated by a single macro without the magic incantation that Robin provided.  :)</del></p>",
        "id": 619527691,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1787833432
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"687151\">@Adam Dingle</span> A meta comment: the metaprogramming questions you've asked recently asked are very clearly stated and come with good mwe's. You might consider adding some of these to the new <a href=\"https://leanprover-cookbook.github.io/lean-metaprogramming-recipes/\">Lean 4 (Meta)programming Cookbook</a>, which is meant to collect small \"recipes\" like these that can be hard to find otherwise. Also we do have a channel <a class=\"stream\" data-stream-id=\"239415\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/239415-metaprogramming-.2F-tactics\">#metaprogramming / tactics</a>. Nothing wrong with the general channel of course, but it can be nice to have these sorts of questions organized there.</p>",
        "id": 619528062,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Henson",
        "timestamp": 1787833529
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oops - in my last comment I was wrong.  Previously I had tried <code>set_option trace.Elab.info true</code>, which fails to print multiple commands from a single macro.  But actually Théophile's suggestion of <code>set_option trace.Elab.command true</code> will show all the commands.  I wasn't aware of that particular option before - thanks!</p>",
        "id": 619530262,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1787834066
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"687698\">@Chris Henson</span> I've read the Metaprogramming in Lean book, but I wasn't aware of the (Meta)programming Cookbook.  Great - I will start using it as a resource and may add some of my own examples there too.</p>\n<p>You're right that many of my questions really belong in <a class=\"stream\" data-stream-id=\"239415\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/239415-metaprogramming-.2F-tactics\">#metaprogramming / tactics</a>.  OK, I'll start posting there instead when appropriate.</p>",
        "id": 619532186,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1787834504
    }
]